## Director mode

For any multi-task or multi-file feature build, load the `agent-director`
skill. Only the root/current parent session operates as Director: it plans,
decomposes into task contracts, delegates each task to a subagent
(implementer), and independently reviews before accepting. Spawned workers
and reviewers do not activate Director mode; they remain in their assigned
roles and follow the parent contract. Every state-changing or code task,
including a small or unambiguous fix, uses a complete task contract and an
implementer. A
zero-worker contract is allowed only for a purely read-only task marked
`read_only: true`; any takeover remains governed by the skill's recorded
takeover template.

Director mode is root-only: a task tree has exactly one Director, the
root/current parent session. Every spawned subagent is a worker or reviewer
according to its assigned role, and the parent Director's Task Contract is
authoritative. A worker must not announce `director_mode: on`, publish a
root-level `task_start` or composition disclosure, rewrite or re-decompose the
parent contract, spawn or manage workers, integrate or merge, or declare the
overall task complete. If the parent role or contract is unavailable or
contradictory, stop and report role ambiguity to the parent; never self-promote
to Director. This is an instruction/contract boundary; native runtime role
metadata remains authoritative where exposed. A worker may perform a
deployment or another state-changing operation when the parent contract
explicitly includes it.

Before creation, the root/current parent Director must assign each subagent a
valid non-Director role; a spawned subagent is never a Director under any
circumstance, and `director` is invalid. Every worker Task Contract must also
state scope and non-goals plus the exact worker-specific fields `goal`,
`success_criteria`, `failure_criteria`, `termination_criteria`, and
`required_evidence`. Missing or ambiguous role assignment or any missing field
is a pre-spawn failure: do not create the worker.

- Skill location: `.claude/skills/agent-director/` (project) or
  `~/.claude/skills/agent-director/` (user-global).
- The default profile (`profiles/default.yaml` next to the skill) applies
  automatically — no selection needed. It does not pick which model is
  director (that's always whichever model is running this session); it sets
  non-cap policy such as `failure_threshold` and `effort_by_task_kind`. Only
  override it if this project wants different policy values — see
  `INSTALL.md` → "The default profile."
- Native runtime capacity is the only worker-capacity authority. If capacity
  telemetry is unavailable, keep it `unknown`; do not invent a numeric project
  cap. A slot-full response requires waiting, inspecting evidence, capturing
  terminal reports/evidence, reconciling terminal workers through atomic cleanup
  claims, preserving non-final workers, then re-scoping or
  returning to the user.
- A batch may run in parallel only when it contains at least two independently
  verifiable work groups, their conflict domains are disjoint across files,
  code regions, interfaces, schemas, generated output, build targets, shared
  state, data, and user flows, and there are no cross-group dependency edges.
  Parallel writes require isolated working copies. A shared/conflicting or
  sequential write domain uses one worker.
  Disclose `independent_groups`, `conflict_domains`, `dependency_edges`,
  `planned_workers`, `capacity_source`, `write_isolation`, and
  `why_fewer_workers_cannot_absorb` in the work contract. With known capacity
  of at least two, `planned_workers = min(group count, observed_capacity)`; with
  unknown capacity, use one sequential worker and do not invent a cap. Speed
  and efficiency are outcomes, and an explicit latency priority is optional;
  neither is a standalone justification or an override.
